Product Requirements Document (PRD)
Battle City AI Game (Tank 1990)

Course: AL2002 – Artificial Intelligence Lab
Semester: Spring 2026
Team Size: 1–2 Members
Language: Python

1. Overview
1.1 Product Summary

Battle City AI Game is a 2D grid-based tank combat game that integrates core Artificial Intelligence concepts including:

Constraint Satisfaction Problems (CSP)
Search Algorithms (BFS, Greedy Best-First, A*)
Intelligent Agents
Adversarial Search (Minimax + Alpha-Beta Pruning)

The system simulates dynamic gameplay where enemy tanks exhibit different intelligent behaviors based on AI algorithms.

1.2 Objectives
Build a fully functional Battle City game
Demonstrate AI algorithms through gameplay behavior
Implement intelligent agents with distinct decision-making logic
Showcase adversarial decision-making using Minimax
Provide analytical comparison of algorithms
2. Game Description
2.1 Game World
Grid Size: 26 × 26 tiles
Coordinate System:
Top-left: (0,0)
X → right, Y → down
Fixed Positions:
Eagle/Base: Bottom center (~12,24)
Enemy Spawns: Top-left, top-center, top-right
Player Spawn: Bottom-left
2.2 Core Mechanics
Movement
Tanks move in 4 directions (Up, Down, Left, Right)
Movement restricted by terrain
Shooting
Each tank fires one bullet at a time
Bullet interactions:
Brick → destroyed
Steel → blocks bullet
Tank → damage
Eagle → game over
Game Loop

Each tick executes:

Player input
AI decisions
Movement
Shooting
Bullet update
Collision detection
State update
Enemy spawn
Render
Win/Lose check
2.3 Win/Lose Conditions

Win:

Destroy all enemy tanks

Lose:

Player loses all lives
Eagle is destroyed
3. Levels Design
3.1 Level 1 — Brick Maze
Dense brick environment
Enemies:
Basic Tanks (BFS)
Fast Tanks (Greedy)
Focus:
Dynamic map changes
BFS path recalculation
3.2 Level 2 — Steel Fortress
Mix of brick and steel walls
Enemies:
Fast Tanks
Armor Tanks (A*)
Focus:
Cost-aware navigation
Strategic movement
3.3 Boss Level — Tank Commander
Small arena (12×12)
Single Boss Tank
Uses:
Minimax + Alpha-Beta
Multi-phase behavior based on HP
4. Tank Types & AI Behavior
4.1 Basic Tank
HP: 1
Algorithm: BFS
Agent Type: Simple Reflex
Behavior:
Moves via shortest path to Eagle
Shoots if player in line-of-sight
4.2 Fast Tank
HP: 1
Algorithm: Greedy Best-First
Agent Type: Goal-Based
Behavior:
Rushes toward Eagle
Ignores player
May get stuck (local minima)
4.3 Armor Tank
HP: 4
Algorithm: A*
Agent Type: Model-Based Reflex
Behavior:
Uses cost-aware pathfinding
Retreats after 3 hits
Replans path dynamically
4.4 Boss Tank
HP: 10
Algorithm: Minimax + Alpha-Beta
Agent Type: Adversarial
Behavior:
Predicts player moves
Uses evaluation heuristic
Adjusts depth based on phase
5. Environment Representation
Value	Tile Type
0	Empty
1	Brick
2	Steel
3	Water
4	Forest
5	Eagle
6. Module A — CSP Map Generator
Requirements
Generate valid random maps
Maintain playability constraints:
Constraints
Eagle must be protected
Path from spawn → Eagle must exist
Spawn distance ≥ 10 tiles from player
Wall density ≤ 40%
Water must not block only path
Implementation
Use:
Backtracking
Forward checking
Validate using BFS
7. Module B — Search Algorithms
Algorithms & Behavior
Algorithm	Tank	Behavior
BFS	Basic	Shortest path (ignores cost)
Greedy	Fast	Rush using heuristic
A*	Armor	Cost-optimal path
Requirement
Differences must be visually observable
8. Module C — Adversarial Search
Boss AI
Algorithm: Minimax + Alpha-Beta
Depth:
Phase 1: 2
Phase 2: 3
Phase 3: 4
Requirements
Implement pruning
Measure:
Nodes without pruning
Nodes with pruning
Speedup ratio
9. Functional Requirements
Player movement & shooting
Enemy AI behaviors
Bullet physics
Collision system
Dynamic map updates
Enemy spawning system
Health & lives tracking
10. Non-Functional Requirements
Real-time gameplay performance
Clear visualization of AI behavior
Modular code structure
Readable and maintainable code